For the synchronization proof, each thread writes exactly one distinct shared slot, then all 96 threads reach the same unconditional barrier. That barrier makes every preceding shared-memory write visible before thread zero reads entries 0 through `valid - 1`; there are no subsequent shared-memory writes, so no second barrier is needed. Input loads occur only for indices below `valid`, shared indices stay within 0–95, and only thread zero writes the output; for zero valid entries, the loop is empty and writes zero. The copy, kernel, result copy, and completion event are ordered in the same stream, and the host retains its buffers until the completion event finishes.

Threads 70–95 skip the entire guarded region in the original kernel: they do not load shared memory, call the barrier, or write the output. Threads 0–69 enter that region and encounter a nonuniform block barrier, violating the synchronization contract; this is undefined behavior, not a guarantee that every GPU must hang. Successful immediate launch checks detect launch-submission errors, not whether threads execute barriers correctly or whether the kernel eventually completes.

Here is a complete CUDA translation unit with a host launch function. It always launches exactly one block of 96 threads, including when valid is zero.

```cpp
// sum_valid.cu
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

__global__ void sum_valid(const float* input, float* output, int valid)
{
    // Contract: <<<1, 96>>> and 0 <= valid <= 96.
    __shared__ float values[96];
    const unsigned tid = threadIdx.x;

    float value = 0.0f;
    if (tid < static_cast<unsigned>(valid)) {
        value = input[tid];
    }
    values[tid] = value;

    __syncthreads();

    if (tid == 0) {
        float sum = 0.0f;
        for (int i = 0; i < valid; ++i) {
            sum = __fadd_rn(sum, values[i]);
        }
        output[0] = sum;
    }
}

// device_input: at least valid readable device floats;
//               may be nullptr if valid == 0.
// device_output: one writable device float, not overlapping input.
// Input must be ready in stream order and remain valid until completion.
// On success, this function has waited for the kernel to finish.
cudaError_t launch_sum_valid(const float* device_input,
                             float* device_output,
                             int valid,
                             cudaStream_t stream)
{
    if (valid < 0 || valid > 96 || device_output == nullptr ||
        (valid > 0 && device_input == nullptr)) {
        return cudaErrorInvalidValue;
    }

    cudaEvent_t done;
    cudaError_t status = cudaEventCreateWithFlags(
        &done, cudaEventDisableTiming);
    if (status != cudaSuccess) return status;

    sum_valid<<<1, 96, 0, stream>>>(device_input, device_output, valid);
    status = cudaGetLastError();
    if (status == cudaSuccess) {
        status = cudaEventRecord(done, stream);
    }
    if (status == cudaSuccess) {
        status = cudaEventSynchronize(done);
    }

    const cudaError_t cleanup = cudaEventDestroy(done);
    return status != cudaSuccess ? status : cleanup;
}
```

