The synchronization proof is direct: each thread j, for j from 0 through 95, writes exactly one shared-memory slot, values[j], before reaching the unconditional barrier. Threads with j < valid write input[j]; all remaining threads write zero without reading input. All 96 threads reach the same barrier, which orders those shared-memory writes before thread zero's subsequent reads. Thread zero reads only indices 0 through valid−1, so every value it reads was initialized by its corresponding thread, with no out-of-bounds input or shared-memory access.

When valid is zero, all 96 threads still write their shared-memory slots and execute the barrier. Thread zero executes zero loop iterations and writes positive floating-point zero to output[0]; no other thread writes the output, and the host does not initialize it. The addition order for nonzero valid is a sequential round-to-nearest accumulation starting at zero, in increasing input-index order.

B’s wait references the first record of E—the record current when the wait was submitted. Recording E again does not retarget that wait, and a query after the second record reports the second record’s status. P2 can therefore race with C1: there is no dependency ordering those two accesses to the shared allocation.

A permitted execution is:
1. A executes P1, writing the allocation.
2. A completes the first record of E, releasing B’s already-submitted wait.
3. B starts C1, while A starts P2; C1’s reads and P2’s writes overlap.
4. The second record of E has not completed, so querying E returns `cudaErrorNotReady`.

This trace respects both streams’ order. The event orders P1 before C1, but does not protect the allocation from P2. Overlapping non-atomic reads and writes violate the ownership invariant; do not assume a particular old, new, or mixed result.

Use two allocations with a ready event and a consumed event per slot. A may overwrite a reused slot only after waiting for its previous consumed record; B may read it only after waiting for its current ready record. First use skips the consumed wait because no consumer has yet owned that slot.

```cpp
// ownership.cu
#include <cuda_runtime.h>
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstdlib>

static void check(cudaError_t status, const char* call)
{
    if (status != cudaSuccess) {
        std::fprintf(stderr, "%s: %s\n", call,
                     cudaGetErrorString(status));
        std::exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }
}
#define CHECK(call) check((call), #call)

__global__ void producer(int* slot, int value)
{
    *slot = value;
}

__global__ void consumer(const int* slot, int* result)
{
    *result = *slot;
}

int main()
{
    constexpr int iterations = 8;
    cudaStream_t A, B;
    CHECK(cudaStreamCreateWithFlags(&A, cudaStreamNonBlocking));
    CHECK(cudaStreamCreateWithFlags(&B, cudaStreamNonBlocking));

    struct Slot {
        int* data = nullptr;
        cudaEvent_t ready{};
        cudaEvent_t consumed{};
        bool used = false;
    };
    Slot slots[2];
    for (auto& slot : slots) {
        CHECK(cudaMalloc(reinterpret_cast<void**>(&slot.data), sizeof(int)));
        CHECK(cudaEventCreateWithFlags(&slot.ready, cudaEventDisableTiming));
        CHECK(cudaEventCreateWithFlags(&slot.consumed,
                                       cudaEventDisableTiming));
    }

    int* results = nullptr;
    CHECK(cudaMalloc(reinterpret_cast<void**>(&results),
                     iterations * sizeof(int)));

    for (int i = 0; i < iterations; ++i) {
        Slot& slot = slots[i % 2];

        // Capture the previous consumed record before reusing this slot.
        // On first use there is no previous consumer to wait for.
        if (slot.used) {
            CHECK(cudaStreamWaitEvent(A, slot.consumed, 0));
        }

        producer<<<1, 1, 0, A>>>(slot.data, i + 1);
        CHECK(cudaGetLastError());
        CHECK(cudaEventRecord(slot.ready, A));

        // Capture this iteration's ready record before its next rerecord.
        CHECK(cudaStreamWaitEvent(B, slot.ready, 0));
        consumer<<<1, 1, 0, B>>>(slot.data, results + i);
        CHECK(cudaGetLastError());
        CHECK(cudaEventRecord(slot.consumed, B));
        slot.used = true;
    }

    // Final drain only: no host synchronization between iterations,
    // and no device-wide synchronization anywhere.
    CHECK(cudaStreamSynchronize(B));
    CHECK(cudaStreamSynchronize(A));

    int host_results[iterations];
    CHECK(cudaMemcpy(host_results, results, sizeof(host_results),
                     cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost));
    bool correct = true;
    for (int i = 0; i < iterations; ++i) {
        if (host_results[i] != i + 1) {
            std::fprintf(stderr, "iteration %d: got %d, expected %d\n",
                         i, host_results[i], i + 1);
            correct = false;
        }
    }

    for (auto& slot : slots) {
        CHECK(cudaEventDestroy(slot.ready));
        CHECK(cudaEventDestroy(slot.consumed));
        CHECK(cudaFree(slot.data));
    }
    CHECK(cudaFree(results));
    CHECK(cudaStreamDestroy(A));
    CHECK(cudaStreamDestroy(B));
    if (correct) std::puts("All results correct");
    return correct ? EXIT_SUCCESS : EXIT_FAILURE;
}
```

